 "name": "Pixelated Solar Facades",
 "description": "Faithful replication of parts 1 and 2 of the online aesthetic survey, using the paper's own screenshots of its survey questions: facade designs for apartment buildings in Trondheim, Norway, clad in coloured photovoltaic panels (FIPV) whose NCS colours are arranged as pixels. Participants answer the paper's background questions (attitude to PV on roofs and facades, PV knowledge, whether they are an architect, designer, planner, fine artist or a student of these, country of origin, country of residence, gender, age group, visit to Trondheim), then rate a colourless multi-story facade prototype (Q10) and the same prototype covered by pixelated reddish PV (Q11) on the paper's 5-level scale from very good to very poor, then choose the facade they like best among one pixelated and two non-pixelated PV designs for the multi-story prototype (Q12) and for the high-rise prototype (Q15). They can leave a comment at the end. Rebuilt from Xiang, Matusiak, Royset & Kolas (2021), Pixelization approach for facade integrated coloured photovoltaics - with architectural proposals in city context of Trondheim, Norway, Solar Energy 224: 1222-1246, Section 6, with images from its Figs. 37-40 (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0; figures unchanged, the question text and answer buttons printed in each screenshot are hidden by cropping the view and shown as live text and buttons instead). The question wording is the paper's. Image ids: ms_prototype and ms_pixelated (answer aesthetic, 1 = very poor to 5 = very good, as in the paper's coding), ms_alternatives and hr_alternatives (answer preferred, A, B or C; the pixelated designs are B in Q12 and A in Q15, per Table 6). Analysis as in the paper: paired t-test of Q10 against Q11, share choosing each design in Q12 and Q15, and means for experts (yes to Q3) against laypersons. The paper (309 participants from many countries, 42% experts) found the pixelated design raised the mean aesthetic rating of the multi-story prototype from 3.28 to 3.64, and the pixelated designs were the most preferred alternatives for both prototypes. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper's high-rise rating questions (Q13, Q14) are left out because their images are not printed, and part 3 (pixelated proposals for real buildings, Q16-Q20) is left out because its photographs of Trondheim are not clearly the authors' own (Fig. 35, used in Q20, is credited to archdaily.com); (2) the optional e-mail question (Q9) is left out; (3) the gender options are ours because the paper does not list them; (4) the question wording follows the paper's screenshots and its list in Section 6, and the introduction and consent text are ours.",
 "author": "Xiang, Matusiak, Røyset & Kolås",
